From f1507b5bd0b8f01117805981b435bd29515b0e5b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justinas Grauslis <justinas@8devices.com>
Date: Mon, 8 Jun 2026 22:33:04 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] QCLINUX: PCI: qcom: force synchronous probe

The qcom-pcie controller probes with PROBE_PREFER_ASYNCHRONOUS. On
robovision the pcie0 leg has RTL8111K NICs whose r8169 probe registers
an MDIO bus; the bus scan makes phylib issue a synchronous request_module()
for the PHY driver, and doing that from the controller's async-probe worker
trips WARN_ON_ONCE(wait && current_is_async()) in __request_module() (benign,
but noisy in the boot log).

Force synchronous probe so PCIe enumeration no longer runs in an async worker,
eliminating the warning. Trade-off: PCIe controller bring-up + downstream
enumeration runs on the boot critical path rather than in parallel, for both
PCIe controllers.
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -2066,7 +2066,16 @@
 		.suppress_bind_attrs = true,
 		.of_match_table = qcom_pcie_match,
 		.pm = &qcom_pcie_pm_ops,
-		.probe_type = PROBE_PREFER_ASYNCHRONOUS,
+		/*
+		 * 8devices: force synchronous probe. The async probe worker is
+		 * the context that makes phylib's synchronous request_module()
+		 * (during the r8169 MDIO bus scan on the pcie0 switch leg) trip
+		 * the WARN_ON_ONCE(wait && current_is_async()) in __request_module.
+		 * Probing synchronously removes that async context. Trade-off:
+		 * PCIe controller bring-up + enumeration runs on the boot critical
+		 * path instead of in parallel (affects both pcie0 and pcie1).
+		 */
+		.probe_type = PROBE_FORCE_SYNCHRONOUS,
 	},
 };
 builtin_platform_driver(qcom_pcie_driver);
